LC)1586934463  aMLRbengerdacMLRdOCLCOdGPOdOCLCOdOCLCFdOCLCOdGPO  apcc  an-us-mn  aGPOO 4aHV6698.M6bC67 2026  a1016-C (online)0 aY 4.G 74/7:C 82/1604aThe Cost of Doing Nothing :bHow Tim Walz and Keith Ellison Fueled Minnesota's Fraud Explosion. 1a[Washington, D.C.] :bHouse Committee on Oversight and Government Reform,c2026.  a1 online resource (53 pages)  atextbtxt2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier  a"March 4, 2026."  a"An Interim Staff Report by the House Committee on Oversight and Government Reform Majority Staff."  a"In December 2025, the House Committee on Oversight and Government Reform launched an investigation into fraud in Minnesota's social services programs that occurred under Governor Tim Walz and Attorney General Keith Ellison's watch. The Committee's investigation has found that senior officials in Minnesota state government, including Governor Walz and Attorney General Ellison, were aware of widespread fraud in federally funded social services programs for years, possessed the legal and procedural authority to stop payments, but repeatedly failed to act. As a result, potentially billions of American taxpayer dollars were allowed to flow to fraudulent actors, while vulnerable populations were harmed and whistleblowers were ignored, sidelined, and retaliated against. While the Committee's investigation remains ongoing, testimony and documents obtained to date establish a consistent pattern: fraud warnings were elevated to senior levels of the Minnesota state government, meaningful corrective action was delayed or avoided, and payments continued long after credible red flags emerged. The Committee's findings raise serious questions about governance and oversight in Minnesota's administration of federal funds. The failure to act decisively in the face of known fraud allowed criminal schemes to flourish and diverted resources away from eligible recipients: the vulnerable populations these programs were intended to serve."--Page 2.  aIn scope of the U.S. Government Publishing Office Cataloging and Indexing Program (C&I) and Federal Depository Library Program (FDLP).  aThis is a publication issued by the United States federal government or produced with federal funds.  aDescription based on online resource; title from PDF cover (viewed April 23, 2026).10aWalz, Tim,d1964-10aEllison, Keith,d1963-10aMinnesota.bAttorney General. 0aLegislative oversightzUnited States. 0aFraudzMinnesota. 0aExecutive departmentszMinnesota. 0aAdministrative agencieszMinnesota.1 aUnited States.bCongress.bHouse.bCommittee on Oversight and Government Reform,eauthor,eissuing body.40uhttps://purl.fdlp.gov/GPO/gpo258817704 zAddress at time of PURL creationuhttps://oversight.house.gov/wp-content/uploads/2026/03/The-Cost-of-Doing-Nothing_How-Tim-Walz-and-Keith-Ellison-Fueled-Minnesotas-Fraud-Explosion_3.4.26_FINAL.pdf  aC0bGPO  abc34 20260806b20260806  aBIBCONEWb20260806  aVEND01b2026082603425cam a2200625 i 00147767720261030133219.0m     o  d f      cr |||||||||||260810s2026    dcua    ob   f000 0 eng c  a1616000379a16175755808 a64-347q(GPO jacket number)  a(OCoLC)1610823932z(OCoLC)1616000379z(OCoLC)1617575580  aGPObengerdaepncGPOdOCLCOdOCLCQdDWPdGPO  apcc  an-us---  aGPOO  a1041-C (online)0 aY 4.C 73/7:S.HRG.119-4970 aY 4.C 73/7:S.HRG.119-497/ERRATA1 aUnited States.bCongress.bSenate.bCommittee on Commerce, Science, and Transportation,eauthor.10aHit the road, mac :bthe future of self-driving cars : hearing before the Committee on Commerce, Science, and Transportation, United States Senate, One Hundred Nineteenth Congress, second session, February 4, 2026.30aFuture of self-driving cars 1aWashington :bU.S. Government Publishing Office,c2026.  a1 online resource (iv, 128 pages) :billustrations.  atextbtxt2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr